      "rationale": "The two tests reconstruct the exact scenarios named in the report: a dot-qualified function call (\"foo.bar(1, 2)\") and a function call aliased without the AS keyword (\"foo() bar\"). Expected values come directly from sqlparse's own documented contracts in sql.py - NameAliasMixin.get_real_name's docstring (\"real name is the component after the last dot\"), TokenList.get_parent_name's docstring (\"parent object is identified by the first occurring dot\"), and NameAliasMixin.get_alias's documented \"name alias\" (no-AS) case - rather than from whatever the currently-regressed grouping code happens to produce. A helper does a structural DFS instead of hard-coding token indices, so the tests fail precisely when grouping drops the Function/Identifier nesting (the reported regression) and pass once that grouping is restored, without being sensitive to incidental whitespace-token layout.",
